public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
    long X = input.nextLong();
    BigDecimal x = new BigDecimal(X);
    BigDecimal divisor = new BigDecimal("10.0");
    BigDecimal div = x.divide(divisor, 0, RoundingMode.CEILING);
    System.out.println(div.toPlainString());
    input.close();
}

fix_code_raw_response:
The issue arises because the `BigDecimal.divide` method in Java performs a division that does not necessarily round up to the nearest integer. Instead, it performs a standard division which can lead to incorrect results when negative numbers are involved. To fix this, we need to ensure that the division operation correctly rounds up to the nearest integer, even for negative numbers.

Here's the patch to fix the issue:

